{"name":"Precious Nectar","hex":"#ffde9c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"precious","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of high value or worth.","Regarded with love or tenderness."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone (or something) who is loved; a darling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/precious"},{"term":"nectar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The drink of the gods. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any delicious drink, now especially a type of sweetened fruit juice."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To feed on nectar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nectar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Nectar","blocks":[{"text":"Nectar is a viscous, sugar-rich liquid produced by plants in glands called nectaries, either within the flowers with which it attracts pollinating animals, or by extrafloral nectaries, which provide a nutrient source to animal mutualists, which in turn provide herbivore protection. Common nectar-consuming pollinators include mosquitoes, hoverflies, wasps, bees, butterflies and moths, hummingbirds, honeyeaters and bats. \nNectar is an economically important substance as it is the sugar source for honey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Nectar.jpg/330px-Nectar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nectar","wikidata":"Q171934"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/500px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/1280px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/500px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orange-yellow nectaries and greenish nectar in buckwheat flowers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABuckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg/500px-Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg/500px-Australian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Australian painted lady feeding on a flower's nectar","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustralian_painted_lady_feeding_closeup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/0rchideen_04.jpg/500px-0rchideen_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/0rchideen_04.jpg/1280px-0rchideen_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/0rchideen_04.jpg/500px-0rchideen_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gymnadenia conopsea flowers with nectar-filled spur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A0rchideen_04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg/500px-Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg/1280px-Extrafloral_nectaries_of_a_Wild_Cherry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/